{#
  Every image on the site renders through here — the engine-owned canonical,
  served as @flatcms/picture.twig. Components call it through a fallback list,
  which is what gives a site one deliberate, site-wide override point:

    {% include ['picture.twig', '@flatcms/picture.twig'] with {
         image: fields.image,
         widths: [640, 960, 1280],
         sizes: '(min-width: 1200px) 1100px, 100vw',
         lazy: false
       } only %}

  No local picture.twig → this file renders, and the site keeps receiving
  engine improvements. Creating theme/picture.twig overrides image rendering
  across every component using the list — video posters included. Inside such
  an override, reference this canonical directly as '@flatcms/picture.twig',
  never via the fallback list, which would resolve to the override itself and
  recurse.

  A template rather than a PHP helper on purpose: building this markup in PHP
  would mean marking a function `is_safe: html`, which is the exact mistake
  §9 records removing the `|rich` filter for. As a template, autoescaping
  handles `alt` for free.

  There is no bare-<img> case to fall back to. Without Cloudflare's
  `format=auto` doing content negotiation, WebP-with-a-fallback needs a
  <source type> on every image anyway — so one markup shape, no per-component
  branching, and art direction later is just adding `media` to a source.

  `alt` goes on the <img> and never on a <source>: sources take no alt, and
  splitting it across them is the usual way to end up with none at all. It is
  read from the image object itself, so a component cannot forget to wire it
  up — and a decorative image already carries alt="" by declaration.
#}
{% set src = image.src|default('') %}

{% if src %}
  {# img() answers '' for a width outside the derivative allowlist, so a
     component asking for 1100px loses that srcset entry instead of shipping a
     URL that 404s. The finite width set is the whole reason an anonymous GET
     cannot mint arbitrary variants; it is not negotiable per component. #}
  {# Nothing above the original's own width: a 640w descriptor on a 600px
     source is a lie the browser acts on, and upscaling costs bytes to lose
     detail. When the original is smaller than every allowed width there is no
     derivative worth making, and the <img> falls back to the stored file. #}
  {% set usable = widths|default([])
       |filter(w => img(src, w) and (image.width|default(0) == 0 or w <= image.width))
       |map(w => w) %}
  {% set sizes = sizes|default('100vw') %}
  {% set lazy = lazy is defined ? lazy : true %}

  <picture>
    {% if usable %}
      <source type="image/webp" sizes="{{ sizes }}"
              srcset="{{ usable|map(w => img(src, w, 'webp') ~ ' ' ~ w ~ 'w')|join(', ') }}">
    {% endif %}
    <img src="{{ usable ? img(src, usable|last) : src }}"
         {% if usable %}srcset="{{ usable|map(w => img(src, w) ~ ' ' ~ w ~ 'w')|join(', ') }}"
         sizes="{{ sizes }}"{% endif %}
         alt="{{ image.alt|default('') }}"
         {#- The original's intrinsic dimensions, so the browser can reserve the
             box before a byte of image arrives. Omitted rather than guessed
             when unknown: a wrong ratio reserves the wrong box, which is worse
             than reserving none. -#}
         {% if image.width|default(0) > 0 and image.height|default(0) > 0 %}
         width="{{ image.width }}" height="{{ image.height }}"
         {% endif %}
         {#- Lazy by default, but the hero must pass false: lazy-loading the
             above-the-fold image delays LCP, and it is the single most common
             way responsive images are got wrong. #}
         loading="{{ lazy ? 'lazy' : 'eager' }}" decoding="async"
         {%- if not lazy %} fetchpriority="high"{% endif %}>
  </picture>
{% endif %}
